<h2>How We Design Commercial Systems</h2>
<h3>Low voltage where it belongs, 120V where it exists</h3>
<p>Most of the landscape layer is 12V LED on brass fixtures, which is safer around the public, easier to maintain and far more flexible than running conduit for every fixture. Where the site already has 120V pole lights or building-mounted fixtures, we design around them, often reducing their output or warming their color so the landscape layer is not overwhelmed. For long community entrance runs we specify multiple transformers positioned close to the fixture groups rather than one large unit at the far end, which keeps voltage drop and future troubleshooting under control.</p>
<h3>Light levels and uniformity</h3>
<p>Commercial design is more quantitative than residential. We target light levels appropriate to each area, typically 1 to 2 footcandles maintained on walkways and 0.5 footcandle minimum at the darkest point of a lit path, with uniformity ratios that avoid bright-dark-bright patterns. Facade and sign lighting uses 2700K to 3000K depending on the building materials and brand standards; 2700K remains our default on limestone and stucco, with 3000K on very white or gray contemporary buildings. Our <a href="/tools/color-temperature-guide/">color temperature guide</a> illustrates the difference.</p>
<h3>Durability and vandal resistance</h3>
<ul>
<li>Solid brass and copper fixture bodies with stainless hardware and tamper-resistant fasteners in public areas.</li>
<li>Recessed well lights and hardscape lights where foot traffic or mowing would damage stake-mounted fixtures.</li>
<li>Locked stainless transformer enclosures and conduit-protected cable at crossings and in open turf.</li>
<li>Integrated LED fixtures with 50,000-hour ratings to minimize lamp changes across large fixture counts.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Controls</h3>
<p>Astronomical timers on every transformer, with <a href="/services/smart-lighting-systems/">smart controls</a> for properties that want scheduling by zone, event scenes or remote monitoring. Multifamily and office sites typically run safety layers dusk to dawn and accent layers until midnight.</p>

<h2>Austin-Specific Considerations</h2>
<p><strong>Dark-sky expectations.</strong> Austin and the Hill Country communities have strong outdoor lighting sensibilities, and commercial properties attract more scrutiny than homes. Fully shielded fixtures, warm color temperatures, light aimed at surfaces rather than the sky and reasonable shutoff times are standard in our commercial designs. They also happen to be what municipal and HOA reviewers want to see.</p>
<p><strong>Heat and irrigation.</strong> Commercial beds are irrigated heavily and bake in full sun. Fixture and connector selection reflects that: brass bodies, sealed optics, gel-filled connections, and drivers rated for high ambient temperatures.</p>
<p><strong>Live oaks and street trees.</strong> Austin protects heritage trees, and commercial sites often have significant live oaks. We light them with tree-safe methods and route cable to avoid root zones.</p>
<p><strong>Caliche and hardscape.</strong> Trenching through caliche and under existing paving is slow. On new construction we coordinate with the general contractor to sleeve conduit before paving; on existing sites we bore or use expansion joints to cross hardscape cleanly.</p>

